---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Security & Privacy — How QuiiCast Protects a Session"
	description="How QuiiCast's peer-to-peer connection, 4-digit codes, and rate limiting actually work — and what to keep in mind before sharing anything sensitive."
	path="/security"
>
	<div class="text-center">
		<h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white sm:text-4xl">
			Security & Privacy
		</h1>
		<p class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-xl text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
			A tool that shows someone your screen deserves a straight answer about how a session is protected.
			Here's exactly how it works.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="prose mx-auto mt-14 max-w-2xl">
		<h2>The connection itself</h2>
		<p>
			QuiiCast is built on WebRTC, the same browser technology behind most video calling. Once two
			browsers connect, video and audio travel directly between them — peer-to-peer — encrypted in
			transit using DTLS and SRTP, the standard WebRTC encryption protocols. QuiiCast's server helps
			the two browsers find each other (this is called signaling: looking up a code, exchanging
			connection details) but the video stream itself never passes through it, and is never visible
			to QuiiCast at any point. There's no recording, no storage, and no way for anyone but the
			people actually connected to see the stream.
		</p>

		<h2>How the 4-digit code is protected</h2>
		<p>
			A 4-digit code has 10,000 possible combinations, which sounds thin on its own — the actual
			protection comes from three things working together, not the code length alone:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Short lifetime.</strong> A code is valid for a limited window (10 minutes by default) and refreshes automatically if the caster keeps sharing past that point, so an old code left in a chat log stops working on its own.</li>
			<li><strong>Rate limiting on guesses.</strong> Attempts to join with a code are capped at 20 per IP address every 5 minutes on the server side. Beyond that, further attempts are rejected outright — a script trying to brute-force the full code space would take far longer than any code stays valid.</li>
			<li><strong>Nothing to gain from guessing right.</strong> Joining a session gets you view access to whatever's currently being shared — there's no account, no stored data, and no persistent access beyond that single live session.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			The caster can also see how many receivers are currently connected, and stopping the share
			ends every connection immediately, for everyone.
		</p>

		<h2>What QuiiCast can't do</h2>
		<p>
			QuiiCast is intentionally view-only. There's no remote control, no keystroke access, and no way
			for a receiver to interact with the caster's machine beyond watching and, if enabled, listening.
			If a use case genuinely requires someone else to click or type on your machine, QuiiCast is the
			wrong tool — a proper remote-support tool with its own consent flow is the right one.
		</p>

		<h2>Sharing something safely</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>Close anything you wouldn't want a viewer to see — other tabs, notifications, messaging apps — before you start, or share a single window instead of your full screen if your browser's picker offers it.</li>
			<li>Never share a screen while entering a password, a two-factor code, or banking details. Pause or stop sharing first.</li>
			<li>Only send a session code to someone you actually intend to share with — anyone with the code and a browser can join until it expires.</li>
			<li>Stop sharing as soon as you're done. It also happens automatically if you close the tab.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>Third-party infrastructure</h2>
		<p>
			QuiiCast's signaling server and database run on standard cloud infrastructure providers, which
			may keep their own operational logs as part of running that infrastructure, separate from
			anything QuiiCast itself collects. Full detail on data handling, analytics, and advertising is
			in the <a href="/privacy-policy">privacy policy</a>.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="glass-card mx-auto mt-14 flex max-w-2xl flex-col items-center gap-5 p-8 text-center sm:flex-row sm:justify-between sm:text-left">
		<div>
			<h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">More questions?</h2>
			<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Check the FAQ, or see how a session actually connects.</p>
		</div>
		<div class="flex shrink-0 gap-3">
			<a href="/faq" class="btn-secondary">FAQ</a>
			<a href="/how-it-works" class="btn-primary">How it works</a>
		</div>
	</div>
</BaseLayout>
